syntax: zsh conditions provided by modules, as a new ModuleTest node

zsh accepts any word starting with '-' as a condition in [[ ]], with any
number of arguments when it comes first and one argument when it stands
between two words, and looks the condition up only when the test runs.
Modules use this to add conditions: zsh/computil provides the completion
conditions -prefix, -suffix, -after and -between, and zsh/pcre the infix
-pcre-match. zsh -n accepts

	[[ -prefix - ]] && return
	if [[ -prefix 1 *@ ]] || [[ -between 1 a b ]]; then :; fi
	[[ $line -pcre-match '^\d+$' ]]

and zsh's own completion functions use the completion conditions in dozens
of files. The parser rejected the prefix form with "not a valid test
operator" for the first argument and the infix form for the operator.

With LangZsh, a word starting with '-' that is not a known unary operator
and is followed by more words before ]], &&, || or ) now parses as a
ModuleTest with those words as arguments, unless the next word is a binary
operator, as in [[ -prefix == x ]]. A word starting with '-' where a binary
operator would be parses as an infix ModuleTest. A lone [[ -prefix ]] is
still a word, the non-empty string test.

Reproducer, before this change: `[[ -prefix -c ]]` with LangZsh failed
with "not a valid test operator: `-c`".

